The minimax principle is a powerful tool that links the existence of solutions to variational problems with critical point properties of a corresponding action functional. This paper specifically examines the existence of solutions in the $H^1_0(U)$ space for a nonlinear Dirichlet problem featuring a nonlinear potential and mixed boundary conditions. To overcome challenges arising from the absence of coercivity, we develop an new approach based on a modified functional and a carefully constructed homotopy. Specifically, we explore a problem of the form \begin{align*}
  \begin{cases}
  -\Delta u = f(x,u) & \text{in } U, \\
  u = \beta(y) & \text{in } \partial^* U,
  \end{cases}
  \end{align*} where $\partial^U u \in H^1_0(U)$, and $f$ is an non-cooperative function. Through the application of our modified minimax principle, we prove the existence of multiple solutions. Furthermore, when $f$ is a single-valued function, we establish the existence of multiple solutions within the minimum mountain pass domain, along with theirRegularity properties. Lastly, when $f$ is a multivalued function, we prove the existence of multiple solutions, provided that certain compactness conditions are met. \par This research was partially presented at the International Conference on Nonlinear Analysis, held at University of Lodz, Poland, during June 2019. \par \textbf{Mathematics Subject Classification}: 35J20, 35P30, 58E05, 58E06, 58K10. \par \textbf{Keywords}: Minimax principle, Existence of solutions, Dirichlet problem, Nonlinear potential, Mixed boundary conditions, Homotopy, Critical point properties. \par Research supported by NSFC(11626101, 11771023, 11971015). \par Authors' addresses: \par Chunfang Wang, School of Mathematics, Southwest University, Chongqing 400700, China; \par cfwang@swu.edu.cn (email). \par  Junlin Zhang, School of Mathematics, Southwest University, Chongqing 400700, China; \par jlzhang@swu.edu.cn (email). \par  Chunhui Li, School of Economics and Management, Southwest University of Political Science and Law, Chongqing 400700, China; \par chli@

Vector-meson photoproduction in the forward direction is investigated in high-energy collisions at the Large Hadron Collider using the proton-lead and lead-lead interaction datasets. A theoretical model, incorporating data from the Fixed-Target case and considering heavy vector mesons as preferred outputs in the quasi-real photon regime, suggests that these preferential productions arise due to the differing longitudinal polarizations of real photons in proton-lead versus balanced spin states in lead-lead collisions. This model successfully predicts a $\Sigma$-factor of approximately 0.4 for vector mesons and provides an initial estimate of the vector-meson production ratio across various vector mesons. This ratio is further investigated using the JETPHYS framework and found to be influenced by the vector-meson mass. Furthermore, predictions for vector-meson production in the fixed-target configuration with respect to centrality are calculated and compared with relevant LHC data and predictions. Finally, predictions for vector-meson production in the fixed-target configuration with respect to parton distribution functions are calculated and compared with relevant LHC data and predictions. \end{abstract}"} 

